  "query_preview": "..\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW:\nNortheast Montana will be in NW flow aloft the next few days\nbetween an upper low over Hudson Bay and northern Manitoba and an\nupper ridge over the Rockies and Pacific NW. On Thursday, a dry\ncold front is expected to move in from the north. This will bring\nwinds up on Fort Peck Lake, Nelson Reservoir, and Medicine Lake,\nand will bring critical fire weather conditions south of the\nMissouri River.\n\nA few CAMs (HRRR and NAMNEST) indicate some elevated CAPE around\n200 J/kg on Thursday night, so there is a chance for an isolated\nshower/thunderstorm along the canadian border.\n\nFriday will be cooler in the north and east while the southwest \nwill have temperatures similar to Thursday being near a quasi- \nstationary front.  \n\nThe GFS and ECMWF ensembles are suggesting a flattening of the\nridge this weekend as it moves through eastern Montana. At the\nsame time, a frontal boundary will remain quasi-stationary along\nthe southern part of the forecast area so that the north will have\nhighs mainly in the 80s while the south has highs mainly in the\n90s. \n\nThe latest NBM delays any chances for precipitation until Sunday\nevening.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\nThe only deviation was to PoPs in the north on Thursday night to \nbring them to slight chance.\n\nThere is high confidence in the Lake Wind Advisory for tomorrow.\n\nThere is low to moderate confidence of precipitation occurring on\nThursday night in the north.\n\nThere is moderate confidence of isolated smoke and haze reducing\nvisibility slightly during the late morning through late afternoon\non Thursday.\n\nThere is high confidence in critical fire weather occurring \nThursday afternoon south of the Missouri River.",
